  deep: "<p><strong>Agent security as the defining engineering challenge — and opportunity — of the autonomous era.</strong> Step back and see the shape of what's happening: AI is moving from systems that ADVISE (chatbots — a human decides and acts) to systems that ACT (agents — the AI decides and acts), and this changes the security problem fundamentally. An advisory system's worst failure is bad advice a human can reject; an agentic system's worst failure is a wrong ACTION taken autonomously, possibly irreversibly, at scale, possibly steered by an adversary. This is a genuinely new and hard problem — it combines the AI-specific challenge of prompt injection (unsolvable in principle, only bounded in practice — 8.5) with the classical security challenges of privileged automated systems (least privilege, sandboxing, blast-radius) — and the 2026 reality is that the industry is still early in solving it. Research pushes on structural defenses (the reader/actor separation you learned, capability-based permissions, formal trust boundaries); standards bodies work on agent-security frameworks; regulators eye high-stakes autonomous decisions. What this means for you: agent security is simultaneously the hardest problem in deploying autonomous AI AND one of the most valuable skills to hold, precisely because the two facts are linked — it's valuable BECAUSE it's hard and scarce. As agents proliferate (and they will — the value is too high to resist), demand for people who can secure them will vastly outstrip supply, because the skill combines things that rarely coexist in one person: deep understanding of how AI fails (this course), classical security thinking, systems architecture, and the judgment to know where autonomy is and isn't appropriate (10.5). You now hold all of these — not as trivia, but as a composable, demonstrated capability. The market position this creates is genuinely strong: while others rush to build agents (easy, commoditised), you can build agents people can TRUST with real stakes (hard, scarce, premium) — and in a world increasingly run by autonomous systems, the person who makes autonomy trustworthy is not a niche specialist but a central, valued, well-compensated professional. The builder phase set out to make you someone who can take AI from idea to deployed, dependable, secure, profitable reality. You are that person. The next three tracks show you what the market will pay for it, and how to make sure it pays you.</p>"
